  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Ceriporia reticulata is a wood-decay fungus in the Irpicaceae family, characterized by its distinctive reticulate (net-like) pore surface. Found throughout New England, it plays an important ecological role in decomposing hardwood substrates.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Reticulate Pore Surface",
      "description": "The most distinctive feature is the net-like or honeycomb-like pore arrangement on the underside, which gives the species its name 'reticulata'",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Cream to Pale Tan Color",
      "description": "The fruiting body typically displays a cream, pale yellow, or tan coloration that may darken with age or handling"
    },
    {
      "label": "Thin, Brittle Consistency",
      "description": "The fruiting body is characteristically thin and brittle, often appearing as small, delicate shelf-like structures on wood"
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Ceriporia reticulata grows on dead hardwoods and decaying logs throughout New England forests, preferring moist conditions in shaded environments.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The reticulate pore structure is so distinctive that it serves as a reliable field identification character, making this fungus relatively easy to identify compared to many polypore species.",
